{:title "Authoring Projects with Spork"
 :template "docpage.html"
 :order 1}
---

Spork includes a program @code`janet-pm` that can be used to build software
with C extensions, install packages, and install dependencies. The
@code`janet-pm` program is compatible with older projects authored for JPM in
most cases, and is able to read both @code`project.janet` files as well as the
newer "bundle" spec built into the interpreter.

## Using a template

The @code`janet-pm` program comes with a few different project templates for getting started. This will streamline setup and help users choose reasonable, consistent project structure and defaults.

@ul{
    @li{@code`janet-pm new-simple-project` - create a project that has no dependencies at all and can be installed and used without Spork.}
    @li{@code`janet-pm new-project` - create a project that depends on spork and uses it to setup several convenience features}
    @li{@code`janet-pm new-c-project` - create a project that has a C component}
    @li{@code`janet-pm new-exe-project` - create a project setup to build an executable}
}

The templates can also be used programatically and further customized with @code`spork/pm` module.

## Simple Project

Simple projects will by default have just an init.janet file, a basic
test file, and a few other things like a CHANGELOG, README, and
LICENSE file.  For specifying build, test, and install hooks, there is
the @code`bundle/` directory which is where both the interpreter and
janet-pm will look for "hooks". A hook is a function that gets called
to preform some generic step of the software development life-cycle,
such as building, testing, or installing a bundle.

Hooks can be as simple as an empty function or as complicated as using a
dependency or external compiler to build C and C++ dependencies. The simple
project template is very minimal, and so can be quite good for projects that
don't require a complicated build step or have a dependence on C libraries.

The trade-off for this lack of functionality is complete independence from
Spork or other helper libraries. Dependencies written in this manner can be, in
many cases, built and installed with just the interpreter. This is a reasonable
starting place for libraries that either have only Janet source code or want to
use alternative programming languages other than C for some of their
implementation.

From shell:
@codeblock```
$ janet-pm new-simple-project a-simple-project
author? John Doe
description? A project
creating project directory for a-simple-project
```

From REPL:
@codeblock[janet]```
(import spork/pm)
(pm/scaffold-project "a-simple-project" {:no-spork true})
```

@codeblock```
$ tree a-simple-project
a-simple-project/
├── a-simple-project
│   └── init.janet
├── bin
├── bundle
│   ├── info.jdn
│   └── init.janet
├── CHANGELOG.md
├── LICENSE
├── README.md
└── test
    └── basic.janet
```

## Default new Project

The default new-project template is similar to the simple project will
create a project that has a @code`project.janet` file in it instead of
the @code`bundle` directory. This will depend on spork (or JPM!) for
building, but will allow us to much more easily add dependencies on C
libraries later or custom build steps. Both JPM and janet-pm have a
rule-based build system that enables incremental builds.  Janet is a
scripting language that builds almost instantly even for relatively
large projects, but C and C++ dependencies will often benefit from
incremental builds.

From shell:
@codeblock```
$ janet-pm new-project a-project
author? John Doe
description? A Project
creating project directory for a-project
```

From REPL:
@codeblock[janet]```
(import spork/pm)
(pm/scaffold-project "a-project" {:c false})
```

@codeblock```
$ tree a-project/
a-project/
├── a-project
│   └── init.janet
├── bin
├── CHANGELOG.md
├── LICENSE
├── project.janet
├── README.md
└── test
    └── basic.janet
```

## "C" Projects

Janet is an extension language, meaning it is designed to both be embedded in
larger programs as well as wrap libraries written in languages like C. Janet-pm
includes a template for building projects that have both a C component and a
Janet component.

The default template for such projects includes both a Janet component
of the source code, @code`init.janet`, and C component,
@code`module.c`. Use this template if you are looking to create
bindings for libraries written in C, or if you know that you would
like to write a portion of the application in C or C++.

From shell:
@codeblock```
$ janet-pm new-c-project a-c-project
author? John Doe
description? A Project
creating project directory for a-c-project
```

From REPL:
@codeblock[janet]```
(import spork/pm)
(pm/scaffold-project "a-c-project" {:c tree})
```

@codeblock```
$ tree a-c-project
a-c-project
├── bin
├── c
│   └── module.c
├── CHANGELOG.md
├── LICENSE
├── project.janet
├── README.md
├── a-c-project
│   └── init.janet
└── test
    ├── basic.janet
    └── native.janet
```

## Executable Projects

Janet-pm also supports a template for building executable
files. Janet-pm has the ability to link Janet bytecode with
@code`libjanet.so` or @code`janet.dll` to create a standalone
executable file. The details do depend on your host C compiler,
linker, and Lib C version, but the end result can be a standalone
program you can copy to other systems and use.

From shell:
@codeblock```
$ janet-pm new-exe-project a-exe-project
author? John Doe
description?
creating project directory for a-exe-project
```

From REPL:
@codeblock[janet]```
(import spork/pm)
(pm/scaffold-project "a-exe-project" {:c false :exe true})
```

@codeblock```
$ tree a-exe-project
a-exe-project
├── a-exe-project
│   └── init.janet
├── bin
├── CHANGELOG.md
├── LICENSE
├── project.janet
├── README.md
└── test
    └── basic.janet
```

### How it works

Any native module imported by @code`init.janet` will be included in
the final binary. The way this works is that during compilation, the
build system tracks which modules were imported to compile
@code`init.janet` to bytecode. Next, we look for the corresponding
static archives installed next to the shared objects we imported, and
link them to both our generated bytecode as well as a C source file
that contains a @code`main()` function.

You can also use @code`janet-pm quickbin myscript.janet myprog` to similar effect without the overhead of a project directory.
